 apps and why they need  cross-site scripting protections  so if tokens aren't enough why do so  many public API is only provide  token-based authentication probably  because the primary use case for these  API is isn't to be consumed directly by  a JavaScript client in a web browser are  you sure your apps are so secure  okay so I'd like to talk a little bit  about state your API is probably not a  museum although I have worked on many a  read-only API myself having been in the  open government space a lot of apps that  just serve up kind of canonical data are  you know read-only api's so this was  kind of an attitude that I had brought  to the work but most likely if you're  interested in this talk your API is more  like a bazaar  it needs to be able to save state and  possibly transactional histories maybe  have object versioning audit logs etc  another quick poll  raise your hand if you develop read-only  api's nobody ok good so something that  people often struggle with in this sort  of development is that client state of  course can't persist across sessions or  devices it's kind of a sort of a thing  to think about but it gets overlooked  occasionally especially when the API  team and the front-end team er have  competing priorities and you know maybe  there's a new piece of data you need to  save and the API doesn't have the  capability to save it yet so there are  considerations around that and just to  say again your your your API is really  not a museum you need to think carefully  about how client interactions should or  should not change your core data a lot  of times API developers have these sort  of grand ideas about how their data  schema works and  what their REST API structure is like  but they're not as prepared to do things  like represent clients state in their  API and you might need to think about  how you do that in other models or other  micro services etc once you have a  client-side single page app and play UX  really becomes important to how you  think about the persistence and refresh  of data you need to consider what data  may change in your API that the user  needs to know about or the app needs to  respond to how will the user get that  data unless you're implementing  WebSockets updates will be based on  polling or strategically timed refreshes  some data can be fetched and persisted  at login others with each page load and  others may need to be after more  granular interactions by the user this  is also another hard thing for API  developers to understand which is that  modern jas frameworks are built on the  idea that the client app is independent  this is especially true under the react  and redux or rxjs model but those single  page apps are really implementing an  independent datastore and data model  inside their own application  so what does that mean well the api's  data model may be similar to but not  identical to what the client needs as a  result getting data  getting data out of the API is easy but  getting it back in will often be harder  because objects that are transformed for  displayed in the client will need to be  transformed back or untransformed to be  persisted back into the API and what  that means for your development team is  that every time a schema of X changes in  your API Y in the client may need to  change its schema as well so a tip here  is to really talk to your front-end or  edge API developers here because their  experience building your data model into  a client can inform a better API design  so the upshot of being static what does  it all mean so at this point of the talk  I want to look at the ramifications of  all these things just to review a URL  refers to the whole of a single page app  any URL referring to something inside  the app beyond the front page is called  a deep link deep linking is possible but  often tricky or annoying to implement  for the front-end devs because single  page apps are often built for people to  come sort of in the front door of the  application single page apps are static  plus logic and Ajax so that means that  your API layer is now exposed to the  world your API can't hide behind  server-side rendering like it did with  with server-side application development  another thing is that all of your  experiences for the client must be baked  into it in the first place  you can't really push a new experience  or flow to the user based on data  circumstance or state it has to be there  from the beginning and by extension of  that sensitive data must be preserved in  the API datum you might not think of as  sensitive but which is data which a  given user might not be privy to needs  to be held on the server side so an  example of that might be things were on  terms and conditions or certain even  error states you might think that it's  simple just to code all your error  states into your application but you you  might not want to reveal all of your  possible error states to the world so  this harkens back to the security  portion of this talk separating business  logic and all possible permutations of  that as well as sensitive data requires  a different way of thinking than you may  be used to so there are some back-end  solutions that can help smooth some of  these difficulties over pretty popular  one is writing edge api's I mentioned  exposing your API to the world well an  edge layer sits in front of your key ap  is and what it will do is expose proxy  and orchestrate your API endpoints so if  there's particular you know REST API  resources that may be very pure for  instance so you have related resources  there may be a number of API calls that  need to be made in all at once in order  to  a particular page of a single page at an  edge API can kind of create a shortcut  for you to be able to pull all the data  that you need in in one view at one time  edge api's can also hold credentials for  specific clients all they need to be  careful with this or other API is that  you don't own within your same  enterprise or externally and then the  reason I said be careful about specific  clients is that it's still possible for  a rogue client to use an edge beyond its  intended purpose so you have to be  careful about that another thing which  muddies the single page app waters a  little bit is that some of the other  frameworks like view and react to  support a form of server-side rendering  they help with search engine  optimization some performance issues on  the other hand they don't really help  with the security concerns that I've  outlined nor with the state issues so  why should you bother to understand all  of this well first of all if you need to  make architectural decisions knowing  better how single page apps works helps  you select your stack so determine your  overall architecture determine your API  is request and schema architecture that  helps us determine your authentication  your security and you should really know  what you're building it's important when  you're working with single page apps for  your API team to kind of have some  guiding principles figure it out  beforehand or how you want things to  work have your team live by them  though iterating is needed so some  questions you want to consider are which  parts of your API are general-purpose  the flip side of that is which ones are  tailored to a given client experience  where do those two things overlap and  what data do you need to protect another  concern is API versioning when you're  building an API to a client coupling  between the API and the client can  become very tight API versioning of  course is kind of a contentious topic  versioning and headers versus URLs is  something a certain subset of devs likes  to argue about I would propose that for  a maximum agility and least code debt  and not having to support multiple  versions in both back-end and client  code that you consider an additive only  approach sort of like when you're  designing a good database migration if  you're familiar with those so backwards  and compatible changes must be made to  new endpoints and old endpoints must  only be removed if no one is using them  anymore  and in case I didn't harp on it enough  just a reminder that we really have to  understand how single page apps function  in order to securely properly secure our  apps I have a suspicion that the world  is probably full of insecure single page  apps and consequently insecure api's so  some things that kind of happen when  you're developing single page apps it's  really easy for your teams to fall into  functional silos because of the the  extent to which the stack is separated  so I'd like to propose some  considerations to counteract functional  silos  I said the word client a bunch in this  talk and I was using it in the computing  sense of a piece of software which  accesses a server but I'd like to  propose an alternate way to think about  that the front end team is your client  their needs will drive much of what you  build listen to them very carefully if  you have a front-end lead they may be  your de-facto Solutions Architect this  was actually the role that I played on  this team because the person who needs  everything and the client to work also  needs everything all the way through to  the backend to work trying to focus your  communication around verticals so when  you fall into functional silos  they're probably horizontal silos by  team and by front-end back-end stack in  that case it becomes more important to  communicate around features so try to  direct your teams around understanding  really front to back how what they're  building is going to work and pictures  and diagrams interaction models with  single page apps tend to be really  complicated at times draw sequence  diagrams they really really help so hey  API developers yes this talk is about  you it's about all of us working better  together  you should understand API eight excuse  me single page apps for making better  architectural decisions having better  security better state management and  better engineering process management  and thank you and happy building  [Applause]  also my team is hiring see any questions  [Music]  I was wondering if you could talk about  some of the trade-offs between a single  page application and just our  traditional rendering application for  some sort of really data-driven like  application that you're gonna construct  yeah well you know I think some things  you need to think about are again having  UX drive some of those decisions for  something that you need to be truly  real-time responsive interactive a  single page app may be a great way to go  but I personally I think that the world  has kind of rushed a little bit too much  in that direction for data heavy  applications I think it's it's it's good  to render things server-side and then  provide Ajax to speed up certain  interactions yeah okay that's it can we  give a big round of applause then -  speaker thank you
 

